{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And now, O Israel, etc.</b> Perhaps it intends to correspond to two things that befell him: the first, in the matter of the striking of the rock, where HaShem told him to speak and he struck it, intending to perform the mitzvah with great alacrity. The second, in the matter of Zimri, where he erred by not mustering to carry out the judgment that zealots may act upon, and he did not remember the sanctification of HaShem's name that HaShem had prepared before him, to rectify the first deed in which he had not sanctified the name as we explained the matter in its place. And although Pinchas judged before him, he was told (Sanhedrin 82b): קריאנא דאיגרתא להוי פרוונקא — the bearer of the letter should be its agent — and Pinchas merited the matter and took the covenant of peace that had been prepared for Moses." "<b>And it is possible</b> that his reason was because his hands had grown slack from the strengthening of hands he had done at the first. Or perhaps he reckoned that HaShem did not say ״zealots may act upon him״ except if he sees it himself; but to seek others to act upon him — no. So he held back and sat and did not act as he did with the rock, where he rushed to strike and a stumbling block resulted for him — and after the truth, he erred in this. Therefore he comes to warn the people of Israel concerning the two matters in which he erred and was prevented from ascending the mountain of HaShem of hosts — a city our strength — and we sat in the valley opposite, etc. — and he said to them, <b>And now, O Israel, hear the ḥukim</b>, which are the commandments that are ḥukim, and this parallels the matter of the rock, where HaShem told him, Speak to the rock, in which he was burned, and this matter would be classified as a ḥok. And his saying <b>and the mishpatim</b> corresponds to the judgments that HaShem assessed in His Torah, which parallel the judgment of one who lies with an Aramite woman, upon whom zealots act — in which Moses erred. And from the combination of both, a decree was issued upon him as mentioned." "<b>And he concluded</b> by saying <b>that you may live and come and possess the land</b> — and let not happen to you what happened to me, that I am not crossing the Jordan. And he concluded by saying <b>you shall not add</b> — to warn them that what befell him should not befall them, along the lines of what they said in the baraita (Torat Kohanim, beginning of Parashat Acharei Mot): Rabbi Eliezer compared it to a physician who entered to see a patient, etc. — and one said to him, Do not eat cold food and do not lie down, etc., lest you die as so-and-so died — this spurred him on more than the first; end of quote. So too in our case, Moses intended to spur them on so that what befell him on account of his adding to strike the rock should not befall them. And he said <b>you shall not add</b> — meaning, what I am telling you, ״hear the ḥukim, etc., that you may live and come״ — this is a condition, that you shall not add, etc.: for if they add as he did in the matter of the rock — they shall not enter, etc., just as a decree was issued upon him on account of this." "And his saying <b>and you shall not diminish, etc., to observe</b> — meaning, when a matter of judgment comes to hand, which he recalled at the beginning of his words, ״and the mishpatim,״ they shall not diminish from it as he did in the matter of Zimri, where he held back from seeking to act upon him and thus diminished the judgment. And it is His saying <b>and you shall not diminish</b> — even if your intention is to observe, meaning, that the diminution would carry an aspect of precaution, to guard against killing the one who lies with the Aramite woman."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:15:07.948989585Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}